{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The stall route is refuted in 8/8 trials. Let me record that and fold it into the parent account."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('../state.json'))\nprec=[i for i in st['interpretations'] if i.get('id')=='d224a7ee6a604e779614bcbd1050240f-interpretation-0'][0]\nIDX=[1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15]\n\nchild={\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"1f661a68c21d426189d35f4a76d491f4-question-0\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"verify_leader\\\",\\\"quorum_size\\\",\\\"lease_check\\\",\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\",\\\"start_stop_repl\\\",\\\"notify_all\\\",\\\"update_last_appended\\\",\\\"verify_vote\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"2e5f37f634834b8197227626ed6d99b7\\\":IDX},\n \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"refuted\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"The question asked whether a leader stalled past its lease can still answer a queued VerifyLeader with success by counting the reachable non-voter, and the execution answers no for this model. In all eight trials (parsed result events at indices 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15) the intended condition was reached: the stalled leader kept Leader state for every sample (leader_not_leader_samples 0 in seven trials and 1 in one) while a different server was observed in Leader state at term 3 between 345ms and 464ms after the partition, so the cluster really did have two servers believing they led it during the stall. The queued VerifyLeader nevertheless never reported success: every endpoint was non-nil, seven trials answering 'leadership lost while committing log' and two answering 'node is not the leader', all at 600-602ms, which is exactly when the 600ms store stall released the main loop. The same run's Apply in the same window also ended with ErrLeadershipLost. \\\"\n  \\\"The endpoints distinguish the two interleavings: 'leadership lost while committing log' is the answer the leader loop's cleanup gives to a verify it has already registered, so in those trials the verify case was served and then the runLeader defer answered it when leadership was given up, while 'node is not the leader' is the follower loop's answer to a request still queued in verifyCh, so in those trials the request never even reached verifyLeader. Neither outcome counts the non-voter's acknowledgement. The reason is ordering rather than eligibility: by the time the stall ends, the lease case is already ready, so the main loop gives up leadership in the next iteration, before the heartbeat round trip to the non-voter can return a vote. \\\"\n  \\\"This refutes the suspicion for this route, not the parent defect: the accounting behaviour that counts a non-voter remains in the source and was observed directly in the parent question's probe. What the trial excludes is the specific consequence I was probing, namely that a stalled leader serving a queued verify hands a client a stale-read verdict. Two limits keep that exclusion narrow: it was measured with in-memory transports, where the acknowledgement round trip is far faster than the loop iteration that gives up leadership, and at eight trials it cannot bound an interleaving whose window is sub-millisecond. The parent question's gap therefore narrows to that scheduling residue rather than closing.\\\"),\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n  \\\"The stall model, not the source, is what the trials refute: a slower or differently placed stall could change which case the main loop serves first, and a networked deployment makes each RPC round trip orders of magnitude slower than the loop iteration, which is the opposite direction from the one that produced these endpoints.\\\",\n  \\\"In two trials the failure was ErrNotLeader rather than ErrLeadershipLost, showing the queued request sometimes never reached verifyLeader at all; those trials measure the queue being drained after leadership loss rather than the race between the acknowledgement and the step-down.\\\"],\n \\\"limitations\\\":[\n  \\\"One execution of eight trials is cited; the endpoints were uniform (0/8 success) but the reported interleavings are inferred from the two error values, not sampled directly.\\\",\n  \\\"The stall is a wrapper around the log store that blocks the leader's main loop inside StoreLogs; the source comment on dispatchLogs shows the store write happens on the main thread, so the model matches that path, but a stall elsewhere (for example inside the lease check itself or a host suspension) is not covered.\\\",\n  \\\"HeartbeatTimeout = ElectionTimeout = LeaderLeaseTimeout = 200ms, stall 600ms, in-process in-memory transports, three voters plus one non-voter with only the non-voter reachable from the stalled leader.\\\"]\n}\n\nknowledge={\n \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@stalled_leader_verify_not_successful\\\",\n \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A VerifyLeader request queued while a leader's main loop is stalled past its lease is answered without success once the stall ends, even though a successor has been elected in the meantime. Across eight trials the stalled leader held only Leader state for every 1ms sample while another server led at term 3 (first seen at 345-464ms), yet the queued verify returned non-nil in all eight: seven times 'leadership lost while committing log' and twice 'node is not the leader', at 600-602ms, the moment the stall released the main loop; the concurrent Apply failed the same way. The endpoints show the lease case was already ready when the loop resumed, so leadership was given up before the non-voter's acknowledgement could be counted, which is ordering rather than the eligibility rule that the verify quorum itself violates.\\\",\n \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Three voters plus one non-voter, the leader partitioned so only the non-voter is reachable, HeartbeatTimeout = ElectionTimeout = LeaderLeaseTimeout = 200ms, a 600ms stall that blocks StoreLogs on the leader's main thread while a client Apply is in flight, in-memory transports in one process.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"verify_leader\\\",\\\"quorum_size\\\",\\\"lease_check\\\",\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\",\\\"start_stop_repl\\\",\\\"notify_all\\\",\\\"update_last_appended\\\",\\\"verify_vote\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"2e5f37f634834b8197227626ed6d99b7\\\":IDX}}\n\nlim=[l for l in prec['limitations']]\nparent={\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"3b823834c8cb46da98e77d8e988bf5d9-question-0\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":prec['source_ids'],\n \\\"executions\\\":prec['executions'],\n \\\"conclusion\\\":prec['conclusion'],\\\"defect_claim\\\":prec['defect_claim'],\\\"rationale\\\":prec['rationale'],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":prec['knowledge'],\n \\\"answers\\\":{\\\"d704b04e706144dc964ff451359eda19-gap-0\\\":{\n   \\\"explanation\\\":(\\\"Still unresolved, with one candidate trigger now excluded by execution. Configuration routes are closed by source (checkLeaderLease counts voters only, and ValidateConfig and ReloadConfig keep LeaderLeaseTimeout <= HeartbeatTimeout), and the timing bound stands: a voter refuses votes while it knows another leader and clears that only in its own campaign path, so the earliest successor is HeartbeatTimeout plus an election round, while the lease removes the leader by LeaderLeaseTimeout + minCheckInterval at the latest. The remaining trigger was a leader whose main loop is stalled past its lease; the child question's probe exercised exactly that in eight trials and reached the condition (a successor at term 3 while the stalled leader stayed Leader, first seen at 345-464ms) without producing a verify success, because the lease case was already ready when the loop resumed and leadership was given up before the non-voter's acknowledgement could be counted. What is left is the sub-millisecond interleaving in which that acknowledgement is counted in the same iteration in which the loop resumes, which these trials neither reached nor bounded.\\\"),\n   \\\"resolved\\\":False}},\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":prec['counterevidence']+[\n  \\\"A targeted attempt at the stall trigger failed to produce the consequence: eight trials with a leader blocked in StoreLogs past its lease, a successor elected at term 3, and only a non-voter reachable all answered the queued verify with leadership loss or not-the-leader rather than success (receipt 2e5f37f634834b8197227626ed6d99b7, result indices 1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15).\\\"],\n \\\"limitations\\\":lim}\n\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Interpreted the stall-route probe and folded it into the parent account. The eight-trial receipt confirms the intended condition was reached (a successor at term 3 was observed while the stalled leader kept Leader state, first at 345-464ms) and that the queued VerifyLeader nevertheless never reported success: all endpoints were non-nil, at 600-602ms, with the two error values distinguishing the interleavings in which the verify had been registered and was answered by the leadership-loss cleanup from those in which it never left the channel. The child question is therefore refuted for this model, a Fact records it, and the parent question's gap narrows to the sub-millisecond interleaving rather than closing; the parent's own defect claim is untouched because the non-voter counting behaviour was observed directly in that question's probe. No execution is requested this turn.\\\"),\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[knowledge],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[child,parent]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False); open('update.json','a').write('\\\\n')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -2\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
